<!-- SECTION 2: THE MPH TRANSLATION -->
<h2>What These Speeds Actually Mean in MPH</h2>
<p>The reason soccer speed is reported in km/h is that Europe uses the metric system and the sport's governing body is based there. For American audiences watching the 2026 World Cup on home soil, the numbers are almost meaningless without translation.</p>
<p>Here is the conversion that matters: <strong>1 mph = 1.609 km/h.</strong> So when you hear "Mbappé runs at 38 km/h," divide by 1.609 and you get 23.6 mph. That is roughly the speed of a car in a 25 mph school zone. It is significantly faster than any speed a typical adult human can reach on foot.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">📏</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Quick conversion reference:</strong> 36 km/h = 22.4 mph · 37 km/h = 23.0 mph · 38 km/h = 23.6 mph · 40 km/h = 24.9 mph · Usain Bolt's world record peak = 44.72 km/h = 27.8 mph. World Cup players are running at roughly 80 to 85 percent of the fastest human sprint ever recorded.</div>
</div>
