<!-- SECTION 6: WHAT SPEED MEANS IN SOCCER -->
<h2>Why Raw Top Speed Doesn't Tell the Whole Story</h2>
<p>The speed rankings are compelling, but elite soccer coaches will tell you that top-end speed is less important than what happens in the 0 to 20 meters before reaching it. In most soccer match situations, players do not have enough space to reach their true maximum velocity — the pitch is 100 to 110 meters long and defenders close space rapidly.</p>
<p>What matters more is <strong>first-step acceleration</strong> and the ability to reach a high percentage of top speed within 10 to 15 meters. Mbappé, for example, reportedly reaches 90 percent of his top speed in approximately 2.6 seconds from a standing start — the fastest measured first-step acceleration in top-level soccer. That is the quality that makes defenders feel him before they see him, not the raw number at peak velocity.</p>
<p>Viníçius Jr. reaches 90 percent of his top speed in approximately 2.8 seconds. Haaland, despite his size, gets there in approximately 3.1 seconds — remarkably fast for someone his dimensions. These acceleration numbers are harder to measure and rarely reported, which is why top speed gets all the attention even though coaches value the first 15 meters more.</p>
